VERDICT
Correct: your code correctly adds one and handles the carry over, but it repeats a lot of logic and uses a slow insert operation.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well: You correctly looped backwards, you understood the need for a carry variable (which you called reminder), and you caught the edge case where a new digit is needed at the front.

Is it correct? Yes, it passes all test cases on LeetCode. There are no failing test cases.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses: There are no logical bugs, but your if and else blocks do the exact same math. The only difference is adding 1 versus adding your reminder. You can avoid this duplicate code by just setting your starting reminder to 1. Also, using insert at the beginning of a C++ vector forces every single element to shift right, which is very slow.

Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason:
Time complexity: O(n) because you loop through the array once, and the insert operation also takes up to O(n) steps.
Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space because you only use a few basic variables like sum and reminder.

Why my approach works: You add 1 to the last digit. If it turns into 10, you leave a 0 and pass a 1 to the next digit to the left. If you reach the front and still have a 1, you push it to the very front.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED

Approach 1: Convert to Integer (Flawed Brute Force)

Where it comes from: Common beginner mistake.

Idea: Turn the array into a normal integer, add 1, and turn it back into an array.

C++ code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> plusOne(vector<int>& digits) {
        long long number = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < digits.size(); i++) {
            number = number * 10 + digits[i];
        }
        number += 1;
        vector<int> result;
        while (number > 0) {
            result.insert(result.begin(), number % 10);
            number /= 10;
        }
        return result;
    }
};
Time complexity: O(n) to loop through the digits.

Space complexity: O(n) to create the new array.

What makes the next approach better: This fails completely because arrays can have up to 100 digits, which is way too big to fit inside a standard integer or long long. We must do math directly on the array itself.

Approach 2: Full Array Traversal (Your approach refactored)

Where it comes from: Standard math simulation.

Idea: Start with a carry of 1. Loop backwards. Add the carry to the current digit. If it hits 10, set the digit to 0 and keep carry as 1. Otherwise, set carry to 0. If carry is 1 at the end, insert 1 at the front.

C++ code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> plusOne(vector<int>& digits) {
        int carry = 1;
        for (int i = digits.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            int sum = digits[i] + carry;
            digits[i] = sum % 10;
            carry = sum / 10;
        }
        if (carry > 0) {
            digits.insert(digits.begin(), carry);
        }
        return digits;
    }
};
Time complexity: O(n) to visit each digit, plus O(n) if we need to insert at the front.

Space complexity: O(1) extra space.

What makes the next approach better: We do not need to keep looping once the carry becomes 0. If we just add 1 to a 4, it becomes 5, and we can just return the answer right then. Also, instead of a slow insert at the front, if we reach the end and need a new digit, it means the number was something like 99. The answer will always be 100. So we can just add a 0 to the back and change the first digit to 1.

Approach 3: Stop Early (Most Optimised)

Where it comes from: LeetCode editorial and top solutions.

Idea: Loop backwards. If the digit is less than 9, just add 1 and return the array instantly. If it is 9, change it to 0 and move left. If the loop finishes, it means all digits were 9, so change the first digit to 1 and push a 0 to the back.

C++ code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> plusOne(vector<int>& digits) {
        for (int i = digits.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            if (digits[i] < 9) {
                digits[i]++;
                return digits;
            }
            digits[i] = 0;
        }

        digits[0] = 1;
        digits.push_back(0);

        return digits;
    }
};
Time complexity: O(n) worst case, but often stops in O(1) time on the very first step.

Space complexity: O(1) extra space.

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use? You used a version of Approach 2, checking every single digit even after you added the 1.

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem? They expect Approach 3. They want to see that you realize you can stop early, and that you know how to avoid the slow insert operation.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:

Question 1: What if we want to add a larger number k instead of 1?

Answer: We would use a while loop that keeps going as long as k is greater than 0 or we still have array digits left. We add k to the current digit, keep the remainder as the new digit, and divide k by 10 to carry over to the next step.

Question 2: Why is vector insert slow?

Answer: Because arrays in C++ are stored in continuous memory. Inserting at the front means every single item must be copied and moved one spot to the right to make room.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

The key insight, in one sentence: If a digit is less than 9, just add 1 and return early; if it is 9, make it 0 and move left.

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time:

Do not write duplicate code; use a starting carry variable of 1 to combine logic.

Avoid inserting at the front of an array if you can just push a zero to the back and modify the front instead.

The pattern or idea this question is really about: Array manipulation and carrying over in basic math addition.

THE BEST SOLUTION

